<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      Trinix.Conformance — `trinix doctor`, as a library.

      Doc 01 on the tool this implements: "trinix doctor matters more than it
      looks. It is where conformance stops being a document." Doc 09 § Submission
      makes a subset of it a gate on a repository submission, and doc 16 runs it
      over Trinix's own applications in CI.

      Three of those four sentences are about somebody *else* running it — a
      repository operator, a CI job, a developer at a terminal — which is why the
      checks are a library returning findings rather than a program printing
      lines. A gate whose only output is stdout can be asserted on with grep and
      nothing better, and the acceptance criterion for a conformance tool is that
      each individual check can be watched failing. Trinix.Conformance.Tests does
      exactly that, one test per check, against bundles broken on purpose.

      ⚠ It references Trinix.Sandbox as well as Trinix.Bundle, and that is the
      most interesting thing here. The checks that are only worth having are the
      ones that answer a question the developer cannot answer by reading the
      format: not "is this JSON valid" but "which of the permissions you declared
      will be enforced by nothing on the system this ships to". That answer is
      SandboxUnit.Gaps, and getting it means building the unit — so doctor builds
      the same unit the launcher would, from the same code, and reports what it
      says about itself.

      The rendering lives here too (DoctorWriter) rather than in the CLI. A
      finding's severity, its one-line reason and its fix are the product; a
      tool whose message does not say what to do is one people run once.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <RootNamespace>Trinix.Conformance</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>Trinix.Conformance</AssemblyName>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Trinix.Bundle\Trinix.Bundle.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Trinix.Sandbox\Trinix.Sandbox.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
